WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.259
Hello everyone and welcome to Unity to Unreal.

2
00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:08.740
This is a course designed for Unreal Engine 5.6 about

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.619
transitioning your workflow from Unity to Unreal Engine.

4
00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:15.119
This course is being brought to you today by myself,

5
00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:17.620
Alex Coulom, as well as Wit Sellers.

6
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.620
We are Unreal Engine authorized instructor partners,

7
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.539
and look forward to teaching you the ins and

8
00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:25.179
outs of going from Unity to Unreal.

9
00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:27.660
So what are we going to be covering today?

10
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.379
First, we're going to get our bearings.

11
00:00:29.379 --> 00:00:30.660
We're going to do an overview of

12
00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:33.479
basic concepts inside of Unity and Unreal.

13
00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:36.420
And we're also going to create some basic assets

14
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.100
and compare those side by side between the two programs.

15
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.079
Next, we're going to work on adding new content

16
00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:46.380
into Unreal, as well as migrating an existing scene

17
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.500
from Unity into our Unreal Engine project,

18
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.179
and look at what kind of assets can come over

19
00:00:52.179 --> 00:00:54.859
and which ones we're going to need to rebuild.

20
00:00:54.859 --> 00:00:56.820
So once we know that,

21
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.619
then we're going to be bringing back

22
00:00:58.619 --> 00:01:00.359
interactivity to our project.

23
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.880
We're going to look at Unreal Engine's gameplay framework.

24
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.400
We're going to rebuild materials,

25
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.519
reincorporate our animations into our characters,

26
00:01:10.519 --> 00:01:12.819
create a new character asset,

27
00:01:12.819 --> 00:01:15.439
as well as script custom logic

28
00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:19.000
using Blueprints visual scripting inside of Unreal.

29
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.319
Then we're going to go into leveraging

30
00:01:21.319 --> 00:01:23.480
some of the tools the engine has to offer,

31
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.200
like lighting with Lumen,

32
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.799
and affecting exposure and bloom with post-processing.

33
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.879
We'll look at modeling tools

34
00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:34.900
or editing some of our assets that we have imported.

35
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.200
And then we're going to start working with audio

36
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.900
and package our game

37
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.480
so that other people can play what we've created.

38
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.760
All right, so here we're looking at a comparison

39
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.159
of our scene in Unity and what it will look like

40
00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:49.319
after we've brought it over into Unreal.

41
00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:52.019
We have an NPC character that's dancing

42
00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:53.680
and the player will be able to switch

43
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.620
what dance they're doing

44
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.579
with the press of a button on their keyboard.

45
00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:01.900
This dancer is surrounded by glowing columns

46
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.299
that have an animated material

47
00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:06.939
and the color of the columns can change

48
00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:08.759
when the player presses a button

49
00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:10.400
on their keyboard as well.

50
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.099
And then in Unreal, we've added in a new character

51
00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:15.659
as well as changed up the environment

52
00:02:15.659 --> 00:02:19.039
and illumination settings to give it a more vibey look.

53
00:02:19.039 --> 00:02:20.400
Okay, so let's take a look

54
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.460
and see what this is actually going to look like in engine.

55
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.449
So here's our scene in Unity.

56
00:02:28.449 --> 00:02:30.389
There we go, lovely.

57
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.669
We have an extra package that we're using here

58
00:02:33.669 --> 00:02:35.590
that gives us a free flying camera

59
00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.389
to mimic the player pawn that comes by default

60
00:02:40.389 --> 00:02:41.669
inside of Unreal.

61
00:02:41.669 --> 00:02:43.750
We'll see that when we press space,

62
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.090
our color of our cubes change

63
00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.590
and the dance that our character is performing

64
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:51.710
also changes up.

65
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.990
If we press space again, color changes back

66
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.729
and the dance will also go back to our original.

67
00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:00.610
Fantastic.

68
00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.449
Now, inside of Unreal,

69
00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:04.289
we're gonna see something that looks very similar

70
00:03:04.289 --> 00:03:06.449
except we've added a few more assets

71
00:03:06.449 --> 00:03:08.530
that we'll cover over the rest of the class.

72
00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.409
We've also added in a new character.

73
00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:12.289
We've got some music playing,

74
00:03:12.289 --> 00:03:14.500
but when we press space,

75
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.219
our dance changes and our colors change

76
00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:26.099
and all of this is a real time lighting.

77
00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:27.580
Okay, so now let's look

78
00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.819
and see how we can take the default Unreal Engine layout

79
00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:32.639
and put it into this layout

80
00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:36.400
that more closely resembles the Unity editor layout

81
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.439
you might be used to.

82
00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:40.780
So we'll open Unreal here.

83
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.039
First thing I'm gonna do is open the content drawer,

84
00:03:43.039 --> 00:03:45.240
which is like our project folder.

85
00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.659
I'm gonna dock this in the layout here

86
00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:49.060
under the viewport.

87
00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.159
Then I'm gonna drag this outliner panel over here

88
00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:54.259
to the left of the viewport.

89
00:03:54.259 --> 00:03:56.020
And now we have something

90
00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.719
that looks maybe a little bit more familiar.

91
00:03:58.719 --> 00:03:59.800
And then again,

92
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.000
we have our main menu toolbar up here at the top,

93
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.000
our play and editor tools right here over the viewport.

94
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.900
The details is gonna be like our inspector panel,

95
00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.680
outliner is like our scene hierarchy,

96
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.920
and then the content browser is like our project folder.

97
00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.019
And then finally, if you don't see your console,

98
00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:26.040
the output log, which is Unreal's version,

99
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:32.110
can be found down here underneath your content browser.

100
00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:33.610
On this slide, we're looking a little bit

101
00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.550
at the Unreal asset hierarchy

102
00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.430
and how different assets nest into each other.

103
00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.730
For example, you might have a world

104
00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.350
that contains different levels or sub-levels,

105
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.350
or in a world partition sense,

106
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.410
you might have a lot of different streaming assets in there

107
00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:51.310
and then within those, you'll have individual actors.

108
00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.589
And then each actor can have various components

109
00:04:54.589 --> 00:04:57.230
or blueprints that can get more and more detailed.

110
00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.389
And then of course, from there, you can go down further

111
00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:01.129
and look at nodes or even code.

112
00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:02.790
But this just gives you a brief overview

113
00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.810
about the relationship to each other.

114
00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.470
There's more detail about each of these

115
00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.850
in the description here,

116
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.949
as well as in the Unreal Engine documentation.

117
00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:13.750
So now we're gonna jump a little bit deeper

118
00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.250
into our program comparison

119
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.029
and look at Unity's scene hierarchy

120
00:05:17.029 --> 00:05:19.910
versus Unreal's level outliner.

121
00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.470
In Unity, the main way that I have found

122
00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.990
to keep my hierarchy organized

123
00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.790
is to use empty game objects as parents

124
00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:29.790
in order to group things together.

125
00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.949
And Unreal, we can still do that with empty actors,

126
00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:36.189
but we also have the ability to group things into folders.

127
00:05:36.189 --> 00:05:39.230
These folders don't actually appear in the level itself,

128
00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:41.269
they're only there in the outliner.

129
00:05:41.269 --> 00:05:44.550
And so they're a way that you can keep things

130
00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.269
grouped together without needing to physically tie

131
00:05:48.269 --> 00:05:51.829
or virtually tie all of their transforms together

132
00:05:51.829 --> 00:05:53.930
under a parent object.

133
00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:58.610
Additionally, the button to add a new engine supplied actor

134
00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:00.750
is moved from the hierarchy window

135
00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:05.750
to the toolbar in Unreal or the main menu toolbar.

136
00:06:06.089 --> 00:06:08.829
And also there is a separate levels window.

137
00:06:08.829 --> 00:06:11.850
So in Unity, if you have multiple scenes open

138
00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.689
at the same time, they'll all appear in the hierarchy.

139
00:06:14.689 --> 00:06:16.569
Whereas in Unreal Engine,

140
00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:20.529
you'll see all of the actors together in the outliner,

141
00:06:20.529 --> 00:06:23.009
but if you wanna see the different levels,

142
00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:27.009
assets themselves, that'll be in a separate levels window

143
00:06:27.009 --> 00:06:30.569
that you'll open and can dock next to the outliner.

144
00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:32.930
Okay, so now we're gonna look at the inspector panel

145
00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.250
versus the detail panel.

146
00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.029
You'll see they look pretty similar.

147
00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:41.029
We have our various components laid out vertically,

148
00:06:41.029 --> 00:06:43.649
but one sort of key difference to note here

149
00:06:43.649 --> 00:06:46.750
is that we don't have a checkbox next to the name

150
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:49.930
of the cube in Unreal like we do in Unity.

151
00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.009
So if you want to disable an actor

152
00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.310
in the similar kind of way that you would in Unity,

153
00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.730
you need to go into the rendering section

154
00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:01.550
of that actor detail panel and search for hidden in game

155
00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:03.889
so it doesn't show up when you're playing it

156
00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:06.769
in play mode or disable the checkbox for visible

157
00:07:06.769 --> 00:07:10.449
if you don't wanna see it when you're working in editor.

158
00:07:10.449 --> 00:07:12.709
So now we're gonna go a little bit deeper again

159
00:07:12.709 --> 00:07:16.509
on prefabs versus blueprints.

160
00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:19.910
Here we're looking at the Unity prefab editor.

161
00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.069
This looks very similar to the default editor layout

162
00:07:23.069 --> 00:07:24.430
for a scene.

163
00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:28.189
We have a hierarchy that can contain sub prefabs

164
00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:31.189
or game objects, each with their own components,

165
00:07:31.189 --> 00:07:33.709
but all logic needs to be added to prefabs

166
00:07:33.709 --> 00:07:38.709
via C sharp script components within their sub game objects

167
00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:42.269
and all of those components need to create links

168
00:07:42.269 --> 00:07:44.930
to one another within itself

169
00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.839
or you manually have to link them together.

170
00:07:47.879 --> 00:07:49.959
Over in the Unreal Engine side,

171
00:07:49.959 --> 00:07:52.040
we have a viewport that is similar

172
00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.740
to what we have in Unity, including the components window,

173
00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.920
which has a little mini outliner,

174
00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.660
not unlike what we get with the world outliner

175
00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.240
in the Unreal Engine level.

176
00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.100
And here you'll typically have a collection of assets

177
00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.920
like static meshes, lights, actors of that nature,

178
00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:12.060
though you can also have child blueprints in here

179
00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.199
nested and containing all the complexities

180
00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:16.740
of that linked blueprint.

181
00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:20.100
However, we certainly advise caution when doing that

182
00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.420
as it can very quickly make the parent blueprint

183
00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:25.939
more complicated and harder to work with.

184
00:08:25.939 --> 00:08:28.180
In the next video, we'll look at how to create

185
00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.139
basic assets in Unreal Engine

186
00:08:30.139 --> 00:08:32.460
and compare them to their Unity equivalents.
